<!-- TOP BAR -->
<div class="top-bar">
  <div class="logo">🌴 Nook Academy</div>
  <div class="progress-track"><div class="progress-fill" id="progressFill"></div></div>
  <div class="bells-counter"><div class="bell-icon"></div><span id="bellsCount">0</span></div>
  <button class="mute-btn" id="muteBtn" title="Mute Tom Nook">🔊</button>
</div>

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-clouds">
    <div class="cloud c1"></div>
    <div class="cloud c2"></div>
    <div class="cloud c3"></div>
  </div>
  <h1 class="hero-title">Tom Nook's Island Academy</h1>
  <p class="hero-subtitle">Lesson Two: Producers &amp; Consumers, yes yes!</p>
  <img class="hero-nook" src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Tom Nook">
  <button class="begin-btn" id="beginBtn">Begin Lesson, Yes Yes! 🌟</button>
  <p class="scroll-hint">▼ scroll down to learn ▼</p>
</section>

<!-- LESSON -->
<main class="lesson">

  <!-- Section 1: Welcome back -->
  <section class="section" data-dialogue="Ahem! Tom Nook here, yes yes! Welcome back to the academy. We have much to cover today, ho ho!">
    <h2>Ah! Welcome Back, My Excellent Student!</h2>
    <p><em>Ahem!</em> Tom Nook here again, your island entrepreneur and part time economics professor, yes yes! I am delighted you have returned for your second lesson. Last time, we covered things like supply, demand, and the difference between <span class="kw" data-term="goods">goods</span> and <span class="kw" data-term="services">services</span>. Excellent groundwork!</p>
    <p>But today, oh ho ho, today we dive deeply into <span class="kw" data-term="producer">producers</span> and <span class="kw" data-term="consumer">consumers</span>. These two groups are the <span class="kw" data-term="foundation">foundation</span> of every economic system, whether you are talking about a tiny island full of cheerful villagers or a large nation using U.S. dollars.</p>
    <p>You may think producers and consumers are simple ideas, but do not be fooled! There is quite a bit of <span class="kw" data-term="complexity">complexity</span> behind them, and by the end of this lesson you will understand how these roles shape nearly everything humans (and raccoons, yes yes) do in the marketplace.</p>
    <div class="nook-tip">
      <img class="mini-nook" src="data:image/png;base64," alt="">
      <div class="tip-text">
        <strong>Tom Nook's tip:</strong> See those golden words sparkling like bells? ✦ Click any of them for a quick definition, yes yes! Each one you learn earns you bells.
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>Let us begin!</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: Who are producers -->
  <section class="section" data-dialogue="Producers! The busy bees, yes yes. They MAKE the things we all use, hm hm!">
    <h2>Who Are Producers? (And Why They Are So Busy, hm?)</h2>
    <p>A <span class="kw" data-term="producer">producer</span> is someone, or some business, that <em>creates</em>, <em>grows</em>, or <em>provides</em> something that others can use. Think of the Abel sisters creating clothing for you and your beautiful friends. Think of someone who builds homes, cars, or technology. Producers! Yes, yes. They make stuff.</p>

    <div class="icon-strip">
      <div class="icon-card"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt=""><div class="label">Create goods</div></div>
      <div class="icon-card"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt=""><div class="label">Provide services</div></div>
      <div class="icon-card"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt=""><div class="label">Build structures</div></div>
      <div class="icon-card"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt=""><div class="label">Organize work</div></div>
    </div>

    <p>Producers may:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Create goods</strong> like furniture, tools, and clothing</li>
      <li><strong>Provide services</strong> like transportation, tutoring, haircuts, or content creators entertaining you</li>
      <li><strong>Grow resources</strong> like farmers or gardeners</li>
      <li><strong>Build structures</strong> like contractors, construction crews, and yes, my team of hardworking Nook employees</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>In my Animal Crossing world, I am a producer when I:</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Craft a wooden bed</li>
      <li>Sell building supplies to you</li>
      <li>Offer home expansion services</li>
      <li>Provide island development assistance</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>In your world, producers include:</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Grocery stores, factories, movie studios</li>
      <li>App developers, restaurants</li>
      <li>Farmers and YouTubers (yes yes, even YouTubers!)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="mnemonic-card">
      <div class="mn-label">⚡ MEMORY TRICK ⚡</div>
      <div class="mn-phrase"><em>Producers</em> MAKE.<br>(Make. Manufacture. Make it happen!)</div>
    </div>

    <div class="quick-check" data-qc-answer="b">
      <div class="qc-label">⚡ QUICK CHECK</div>
      <div class="qc-question">Which of these is acting as a <strong>producer</strong>?</div>
      <div class="qc-options">
        <button data-qc="a">A kid buying ice cream</button>
        <button data-qc="b">A baker making bread</button>
        <button data-qc="c">A shopper checking prices</button>
      </div>
      <div class="qc-feedback"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: Production Chain -->
  <section class="section" data-dialogue="A production chain! One producer needs another, who needs another. Like dominoes of helpfulness, yes yes!">
    <h2>Challenge Word: Production Chain</h2>

    <div class="challenge-word">
      <span class="stamp">CHALLENGE WORD</span>
      <h4>✨ Production Chain</h4>
      <p>Many producers do not work alone. They belong to a <span class="kw" data-term="production chain">production chain</span>, meaning each step depends on other producers.</p>
    </div>

    <p>For example, follow this loaf of bread:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>🌾 A <strong>baker</strong> needs wheat from a farmer</li>
      <li>🚜 The <strong>farmer</strong> needs fertilizer from a supplier</li>
      <li>📦 The <strong>supplier</strong> needs raw materials from somewhere else</li>
    </ul>
    <p>And suddenly, poof! You see that one loaf of bread actually requires multiple producers working together.</p>

    <img class="embed-image" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Production chain diagram showing many connected steps" style="max-width:520px;">
    <p class="image-caption">Producers are connected to many other producers in a web of work, yes yes!</p>

    <p>A good economy depends on producers being <span class="kw" data-term="efficient">efficient</span>, <span class="kw" data-term="organized">organized</span>, and able to <span class="kw" data-term="adapt">adapt</span> when circumstances change.</p>

    <div class="think-box">
      <div class="label">💭 Think About It</div>
      Pick your favorite snack. How many producers do you think helped make it? (Try to name at least three!)
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: Consumers -->
  <section class="section" data-dialogue="Consumers! That is us when we BUY things. Even me, Tom Nook, am a consumer sometimes, hm hm!">
    <h2>Who Are Consumers? (Hint: Everyone, including you!)</h2>
    <p>A <span class="kw" data-term="consumer">consumer</span> is anyone who <strong>buys</strong>, <strong>uses</strong>, or <strong>benefits from</strong> a good or service.</p>

    <img class="embed-image" src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Consumer with shopping bags" style="max-width:160px;">

    <p>Consumers include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>👨‍👩‍👧 Families shopping for groceries</li>
      <li>🎮 Kids buying toys</li>
      <li>🎣 Villagers purchasing fishing rods</li>
      <li>📚 Teachers downloading educational apps</li>
      <li>🌳 Even someone using something for <em>free</em>, like a public park or an Animal Crossing festival, still <em>consumes</em> the service</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In truth, nearly everyone is a consumer. I am a producer when I run my store, but I am a consumer when I buy materials, decorative rugs, or snacks. <em>A shopkeeper has needs too, yes yes!</em></p>

    <div class="mnemonic-card">
      <div class="mn-label">⚡ MEMORY TRICK ⚡</div>
      <div class="mn-phrase"><em>Producers</em> MAKE. <em>Consumers</em> TAKE.<br>(Make / Take. Easy to remember, hm hm!)</div>
    </div>

    <div class="quick-check" data-qc-answer="c">
      <div class="qc-label">⚡ QUICK CHECK</div>
      <div class="qc-question">Which person is acting as a <strong>consumer</strong>?</div>
      <div class="qc-options">
        <button data-qc="a">A farmer planting corn seeds</button>
        <button data-qc="b">A tailor sewing a jacket</button>
        <button data-qc="c">A kid renting a movie</button>
      </div>
      <div class="qc-feedback"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: Consumption Patterns -->
  <section class="section" data-dialogue="Hm hm! What people buy changes over time. This is called a consumption pattern, yes yes!">
    <h2>Challenge Word: Consumption Patterns</h2>

    <div class="challenge-word">
      <span class="stamp">CHALLENGE WORD</span>
      <h4>✨ Consumption Patterns</h4>
      <p><span class="kw" data-term="consumption patterns">Consumption patterns</span> refer to how consumers choose what to buy over time.</p>
    </div>

    <p>For example:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>🌸 If many villagers begin buying more flower seeds than tools, the island's consumption pattern has shifted.</li>
      <li>📀 If people in the real world buy fewer DVDs and more streaming services, that is a change in consumption patterns.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Understanding these patterns helps producers plan what to make, and sometimes what to <em>stop</em> making. (If no one buys my seaweed wallpaper anymore, I must discontinue it. Even Tom Nook must accept reality, yes yes.)</p>

    <img class="embed-image" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Chart showing how spending categories changed during COVID lockdown">
    <p class="image-caption">A real example! How people spent money during the COVID-19 lockdown. Dining (pink) and Leisure (blue) dropped a LOT when people stopped going out. Medicine (tan) went up. By the end, things got close to normal again, but not exactly. <strong>Consumption patterns can shift quickly when life changes!</strong></p>

    <div class="think-box">
      <div class="label">💭 Think About It</div>
      Has your own consumption pattern changed in the last year? Maybe a new hobby, a new favorite snack, or a game you stopped buying?
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 6: How they depend -->
  <section class="section" data-dialogue="They need each other, yes yes! Producers without consumers? Empty shelves. Without producers? Hungry villagers, ho ho!">
    <h2>How Producers and Consumers Depend on Each Other</h2>
    <p>Now we reach the most important part, the <strong>relationship</strong> between producers and consumers. They rely on one another for the economy to function smoothly.</p>

    <h3>🛒 Consumers Need Producers</h3>
    <p>Because consumers cannot make everything they want. A villager cannot craft a refrigerator out of thin air. A family cannot farm every food they eat or build every product they use.</p>
    <p>Consumers depend on producers for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Variety</strong> of goods</li>
      <li><strong>Quality</strong> of items and services</li>
      <li><strong>Prices</strong> they can afford</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>🏪 Producers Need Consumers</h3>
    <p>Without consumers, producers would have no reason to produce anything. If no villager on the island buys my tools, why should I stock them? My shelves would just gather dust!</p>
    <p>Producers depend on consumers for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="kw" data-term="revenue">Revenue</span>, meaning bells or dollars that allow producers to stay in business</li>
      <li><strong>Feedback</strong>, so they know what people actually want</li>
      <li><span class="kw" data-term="market signals">Market signals</span>, information that helps them decide what to produce next</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="quick-check" data-qc-answer="a">
      <div class="qc-label">⚡ QUICK CHECK</div>
      <div class="qc-question">If no one in town ever buys lemonade, what should the lemonade producer do?</div>
      <div class="qc-options">
        <button data-qc="a">Switch to making something people want</button>
        <button data-qc="b">Make twice as much lemonade tomorrow</button>
        <button data-qc="c">Hide all the lemonade in a cave</button>
      </div>
      <div class="qc-feedback"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 7: Interdependence -->
  <section class="section" data-dialogue="Interdependence! Big word, simple idea. We all rely on each other. Even when we never meet, yes yes!">
    <h2>Challenge Word: Interdependence</h2>

    <div class="challenge-word">
      <span class="stamp">CHALLENGE WORD</span>
      <h4>✨ Interdependence</h4>
      <p><span class="kw" data-term="interdependence">Interdependence</span> means both groups depend on each other. Just like each part of a team must work together, producers and consumers form a network of cooperation, even when they do not talk directly.</p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Interdependence</strong> keeps the whole economic system running smoothly, from my tiny shop to the biggest city in the world.</p>

    <div class="mnemonic-card">
      <div class="mn-label">⚡ MEMORY TRICK ⚡</div>
      <div class="mn-phrase">INTER = between<br>DEPENDENCE = needing.<br><em>"Between us, we need each other."</em></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 8: Decisions / Opportunity Cost -->
  <section class="section" data-dialogue="Every choice has a cost, yes yes. Not just bells! What you give up is the opportunity cost, hm hm!">
